"""Leading with AI (Leadership Edition), Session 3: Is it any good? Accuracy claims and the cost of mistakes.  Written 12 November 2026.

The pattern of the pilots: recall of Session 2 and a hook with a real case; the idea as a ladder of icon grids and counts with no maths
(two kinds of mistake, why 95% can be useless for rare events, two better questions, the cut-off as a business dial, pricing mistakes in
shillings, testing on unseen cases, bias); the six-industry tour and three case stories; the recorded invoice-risk demonstration; your
people; the vendor question sheet; the portfolio hands-on; the decision; the take-away.

Cases (research/*.md): HLT-05 Kenya SHA (hook, and the hospital in the customer slide), HLT-12 Optum (bias), tour BNK-08, TEL-05, AGR-01,
HLT-03, PUB-14, case stories HLT-11 Epic Sepsis Model, TEL-05 Airtel spam alert, HLT-08 South African medical schemes; BNK-13 Apple Card
(the customer wrongly flagged).  HLT-03 replaces nothing; it is added to the tour from the case bank (tagged S3) because it gives a
Kenyan example of a high catch rate with more false alarms.
Demonstration: the recorded runs in demo_runs/s03/ (s03_measure.py and s03_threshold.py; scikit-learn 1.9.1; recorded 6 November 2026;
fixed seeds; threshold script run 4 times, including the --miss 100000 and --miss 600000 variants).  The 3,000 invoices are FICTIONAL
(Nile Valley Foods, a fictional company).  The cost per mistake is ASSUMED for the lesson.  No formula is shown anywhere: counts and
shillings only.  Transfer, claim and group counts on the concept slides are illustrative and labelled so.
"""

