    word_icons=['bell', 'cross', 'check', 'switch', 'people'],
    words_note='Five words for today. A false positive is a false alarm. A false negative is a miss. Accuracy is the share of all cases a system gets right, and today shows how it can mislead. The threshold is the cut-off from Session 2. And bias is when the mistakes fall more often on one group. We start from the cut-off you met last time.',
    cover_note='Welcome back. In Session 2 a model learned from past records, gave each new case a score, and someone in your organisation set the cut-off. Today we ask whether such a system is any good. Every vendor will tell you a number, often ninety-five per cent accurate, so our question is this: how do I judge a vendor who says 95% accurate, and what will its mistakes cost us? We start with Kenya’s health insurer, build the idea with pictures of people and counts, tour six industries, and price the mistakes of a recorded model in shillings.',
    outcome_note='That question breaks into three things you will be able to do by the end. You will name the two ways a system can be wrong, and say why ninety-five per cent can be useless when the thing you look for is rare. You will treat the cut-off as a business dial and set it by pricing each mistake in shillings. And you will hold the questions that test a vendor’s claim, with a cost-of-a-mistake line on each entry in your portfolio.',
    keep_note='Keep the question sheet. It holds the two kinds of mistake, the two questions to ask in place of accuracy, the dial, the price of each mistake, and today’s recorded numbers: doing nothing scored 91 in 100 and caught no late invoice, and the cheapest setting moved when the price of a miss moved. Every later tool in this course is judged this way. In Session 4 we turn to language: sorting, searching, translating and summarising, and we judge it with the same two questions.',
